WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:12.960
I invite the Board to move on to the next
item, which is the NHS performance report.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.160
Barbara, I invite you to introduce it for
us.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.240
Thank you, Chairman.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.240
I would like to do three things for you, both
through the report and through what I say

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.760
in the next five or ten minutes or so.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.940
One is to report the performance of the NHS.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:38.140
The second is to reiterate and give you assurance
on a range of actions that we have in place

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:43.780
with our partners in NHS Improvement to ensure
that performance remains the best it possibly

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.050
can be, and to ensure that we are resilient,
especially for the winter.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.940
The third, if I can, is to go on and give
the Board some specific updates and assurances

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:58.870
about the actions we have in place for our
emergency planning role, particularly in the

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:04.190
light of the still potential junior doctors’
action and the events after Paris.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:10.609
I will start by reminding the Board that these
figures demonstrate just how busy the NHS

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:11.649
is.

00:01:11.649 --> 00:01:17.869
Now, immediately, we have hundreds of thousands
of staff out there in the NHS, dealing with

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:21.009
seeing patients, who are very busy indeed
and very stretched.

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:27.200
We have thousands of patients within NHS England
and within our colleagues in NHSI doing everything

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.149
they can at the national and regional level
to support them.

00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:32.429
The numbers are disappointing.

00:01:32.429 --> 00:01:37.979
They will show that there are significant
numbers of standards for care for patients

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:40.369
that we have set and are not quite reaching.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:47.090
However, I will reiterate, as I do at every
Board meeting, that these are small dips below

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.069
the standard we have set ourselves.

00:01:49.069 --> 00:01:54.490
The vast majority of patients in England,
whether they are having emergency care or

00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:00.229
planned elective care, are seen within or
treated within the times and standards that

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:03.210
are set for them in the constitution.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:09.660
Nonetheless, this is still a particular effort
of focus to us, because we know that for patients

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.950
out there, what matters to them is that they
can be seen quickly and effectively by their

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:19.629
general practitioner, they can be seen quickly
and efficiently if they need an ambulance,

00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:22.920
if they need to call 111, or if they need
go to the A&amp;E department.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.120
Where they have planned care, what matters
to them is that those arrangements can be

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.750
made for them at a time that suits them, and
where they do not have to wait.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.920
We continue to focus really hard on these
day-to-day operational issues which are so

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:35.920
important for patients.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:43.220
Our particular focus, and I will therefore
go first to urgent and emergency care, is

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:49.739
to make sure that we are really resilient,
both for the Christmas period and for the

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:54.170
worst of winter, which, particularly looking
at today’s weather, is certainly going to

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.620
get worse than it is at the moment.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:02.790
The report includes a number of high-impact
changes in a number of areas where we are

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:03.799
helping the service.

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:10.120
However, I think we need to focus on a number
of specific issues, making sure that those

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.170
high-impact changes are implemented in every
health economy across the system.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:22.409
We have the emergency care intensive support
scheme working with 28 health economies, sharing

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:27.600
best practice and helping them to put in place
all the systems and processes which will maintain

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.579
the flows through hospitals and make sure
that patients who do not need to go to hospital

00:03:31.579 --> 00:03:36.950
and can be treated in the community are treated
out in the community.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:41.910
There is a particular focus on delayed transfers
of care.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:47.930
You will notice in the numbers that specifically
the social care transfers of care are rising,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.560
and this does give us a problem.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.719
This is not good for patients, because if
you do not need to be in hospital, people

00:03:54.719 --> 00:03:55.719
do not want to be.

00:03:55.719 --> 00:04:01.740
They want to be back in their place of residence,
even if in the long term that is to be a residential

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.390
or care home.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:09.329
We are putting a particular focus on how we
can help not only with the NHS delayed transfers

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:14.269
of care, but working with our colleagues through
DH and local government and social services

00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:22.010
to try and minimise the number of delayed
transfers of care because of social services.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:31.500
We have identified 55 health economies where
we are particularly worried about the pressure

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.220
on them around emergency care.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:40.370
We are working very closely with them, as
I say, again with our colleagues in NHS Improvement.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:45.811
One of the things we are determined to do
is to say to those health economies that they

00:04:45.811 --> 00:04:51.830
should consider slowing down, or they should
in fact indeed slow down, the elective care

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:55.610
that they are offering to patients through
late December, in the run up to Christmas

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:00.760
and over the Christmas period, simply because
what is critically important is that we have

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:04.660
absolute resilience for urgent and emergency
care.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.452
This is not just about the numbers and standards,
although they are important because they are

00:05:08.452 --> 00:05:10.160
a barometer of what we are doing.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.161
It is about making sure that the NHS is prepared
for the peaks and troughs; the spikes where

00:05:15.161 --> 00:05:20.060
we get sudden surges in need for emergency
care, and that, at those times, we can absolutely

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.440
guarantee that all patients will be treated
safely.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:29.540
So for those 55, as I say, we have conveyed
to them that they should reduce their elective

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:30.540
care.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:35.970
I think it is important to report to the Board
that, on that basis, we must assume that the

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:44.070
18-week referral-to-treatment target for December
and January is not going to be achieved, exacerbated

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.540
to some limited extent by the junior doctors’
threatened action.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:52.330
I know it was called off, but a significant
number of operations were cancelled in preparation

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:53.560
for that.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:59.130
When you add all that together, our determination
to focus on emergency care and make sure that

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:04.970
patients get safe services over the holiday
period and immediately afterwards means that

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:09.580
we will see some dip in the standards for
elective care.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:14.220
Moving on to those standards for elective
care, what I think is important to say is

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.350
that we need to see this as a temporary reduction.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:25.570
Our focus on this particular standard in the
longer term is no less determined.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:31.140
It was a really hard-won fight, over many,
many years, to ensure that patients waiting

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:34.940
for elective care were treated quickly and
swiftly.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:40.640
It is important to reiterate that it is not
that many years ago that some patients died

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.780
while they were waiting for heart surgery;
patients went through years and years of disability

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.100
waiting for joint surgery; patients spent
years waiting for cataracts when they could

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.250
not see.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:58.340
Over the years, the NHS fought really hard
to make sure that we had a really strong standard.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:04.170
I cannot remind people enough that it is not
18 weeks until you get seen by a consultant;

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:08.950
it is 18 weeks from the day you are referred
to the date at which your definitive treatment

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:10.190
is in place.

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:11.510
This is a great standard.

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:14.080
The vast majority of patients still receive
this standard.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.230
In fact, the median time is much shorter than
that.

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:22.360
While we need to focus on emergency care over
Christmas and the early New Year, we will

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:28.010
be coming back really strongly with NHS Improvement
in 2016 to make sure that we get back up to

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:33.540
speed, and that we get back to achieving that
referral-to-treatment target.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:34.830
Cancer is so important to everyone.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:38.290
The really good news you will note from the
report is that all the standards have somewhat

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:39.290
improved.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:42.850
We are still not achieving all of them, but
we are making good progress, with a lot of

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:44.780
initiatives in place.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:50.550
I am pleased to report to the Board that our
mandate for dementia, which was the two-thirds

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:55.250
target, was delivered in November, so we have
done well on that.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:08:00.670
We are still seeing a lot of initiatives around
the recovery process on IAPT.

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:05.180
We have achieved the access standard, but
we want to see more patients who receive IAPT

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:06.820
being assured of recovery.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.910
Lots of work still going on on that.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:13.220
Finally, Chairman, if I can just take the
opportunity to talk about emergency planning.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:21.140
It is so important, and this Board has a very
specific role should an emergency arise.

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:27.290
Most of that role is about being prepared
should the emergency arise, and the huge amount

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:32.959
of work that goes on on a day-to-day, week-to-week,
year-to-year basis to make sure that we in

00:08:32.959 --> 00:08:36.680
NHS England – and the whole of the NHS,
because our responsibility is the whole of

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:40.849
the NHS – are absolutely prepared should
any sort of emergency arrive.

00:08:40.849 --> 00:08:48.190
I commend everyone in north Cumbria, where
people within the NHS have had to put in very

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:52.939
significant extra effort to help support the
problems that there have been there.

00:08:52.939 --> 00:08:55.829
We are focusing now specifically on two areas.

00:08:55.829 --> 00:08:58.589
As I said, the junior doctors’ potential
action.

00:08:58.589 --> 00:09:03.910
I need to remind people that the potential
for action still exists.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:09.070
Therefore, we are using this extra time – if
we have it, much though we hope that negotiations

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:14.029
will mean that it is not necessary – to
make sure that we are thoroughly prepared.

00:09:14.029 --> 00:09:19.720
Bruce and I chair a specific oversight group
to look at how we deal with any action that

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:21.459
might come about.

00:09:21.459 --> 00:09:26.079
We were reassured by the plans that we got
in when we thought we had potential action

00:09:26.079 --> 00:09:32.339
for December, in that even the prospect of
moving to the place where the junior doctors

00:09:32.339 --> 00:09:37.240
withdrew their support for urgent and emergency
care, when we looked at the returns we got

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:41.550
from all the trusts across England a very
small number were reporting that they would

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:47.400
have particular difficulties where they could
not continue to deliver safe services for

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.079
patients should that happen.

00:09:49.079 --> 00:09:53.389
Obviously, through our colleagues within NHS
Improvement, we have been continuing to work

00:09:53.389 --> 00:09:59.620
with those seven trusts to make sure that
they could – and the number is now much

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:04.029
smaller – provide the urgent and emergency
care services.

00:10:04.029 --> 00:10:08.069
We have done a number of things nationally
to try and help everyone.

00:10:08.069 --> 00:10:12.089
There is a document which we will be putting
on our website, hopefully within the next

00:10:12.089 --> 00:10:17.850
couple of days, and which will go into the
BMJ, about the various good-practice issues

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:23.940
that you can do under those circumstances
in order to support services outside hospitals,

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.110
to make sure patients do not go there if they
do not need to, and services within hospitals

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:32.769
to make sure that they can be more efficient
should we have strike action.

00:10:32.769 --> 00:10:39.050
What we will do, of course, in the event that
strike action comes back again, is we will

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:43.480
need to go round again, because a small number
of trusts were having problems.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:46.139
Some of it was in the context of that time.

00:10:46.139 --> 00:10:52.569
Some of it was because, for example, one particular
trust had had an outbreak of norovirus just

00:10:52.569 --> 00:10:54.649
before we received the returns.

00:10:54.649 --> 00:10:57.840
So we need to go back around, because actually
the information we need from the trusts has

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:01.220
to be absolutely up to date, and we will continue
to do that.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:07.610
In terms of the unfortunate events in Paris,
I do need to say, because this is important,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:12.980
that the risk level as defined by the Joint
Terrorism Analysis Centre for us is unchanged.

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:17.589
However, that does not alter that fact that,
on the back of the sad events in Paris, we

00:11:17.589 --> 00:11:22.040
have really redoubled our efforts and focus
to make sure that we are prepared.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:28.550
I think our immediate learning from Paris
was about gunshot wounds, and Bruce and his

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:33.711
team – Keith Willett et al – have done
a huge amount of work in identifying and working

00:11:33.711 --> 00:11:40.089
with the experts around the country, making
sure that we are in a better position clinically

00:11:40.089 --> 00:11:44.629
to deal with an episode where there are significant
gunshot wounds.

00:11:44.629 --> 00:11:51.690
Again, I remind the Board that we took part
in a cross government exercise, Exercise Strong

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:55.520
Tower, which I reported to you a couple of
times ago and which was specifically about

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:57.230
a marauding terrorist attack.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:01.509
So we do have recent experience of a very
specific exercise which was undertaken in

00:12:01.509 --> 00:12:02.509
London.

00:12:02.509 --> 00:12:09.249
As I say, the early learning from Paris has
been specifically about gunshot wounds.

00:12:09.249 --> 00:12:17.350
We have also written to all provider responders
– all the acute trusts, and our local resilience

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:18.350
forum.

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:21.910
I wrote to them all, asking that they please
ensure that they reviewed all their processes.

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:26.050
They are doing this anyway, because this is
the time of year when we are collecting all

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:29.550
the evidence so that we can come back to you
as a Board in March, to give assurance that

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:34.759
we have all bases covered on emergency planning,
but they are redoubling their efforts and

00:12:34.759 --> 00:12:40.629
reviewing that; particularly around cascade,
and ensuring that they would be able to get

00:12:40.629 --> 00:12:46.329
all the relevant clinical staff into the hospital
quickly; looking at that where there might

00:12:46.329 --> 00:12:49.439
potentially have been access or transport
problems.

00:12:49.439 --> 00:12:53.839
A particular focus I asked them to do was
on prolonged critical care, because what has

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:58.079
become apparent from Paris is that you need
a much more prolonged ability to provide critical

00:12:58.079 --> 00:13:03.370
care for a high number of patients, as I have
said, around ballistic injuries.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:09.980
The letter also went to ambulance trusts,
of course, asking them to redouble their efforts

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:16.029
and make absolutely sure that the teams they
have who are specifically for marauding terrorists

00:13:16.029 --> 00:13:22.339
and firearms, and their teams specifically
for hazardous substances, were absolutely

00:13:22.339 --> 00:13:24.139
resilient on those.

00:13:24.139 --> 00:13:28.379
This is on a day-to-day basis; this is the
kind of thing that we look at on a 12-hour

00:13:28.379 --> 00:13:31.490
basis to make sure that we are always up to
speed.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.990
We will continue to learn lessons from Paris.

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:35.350
Many of them will be cross-government.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:41.230
There is a big piece of work that we are involved
in with the Department of Health and others

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:45.490
across government which will look at things
like logistics, communications, transport

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:48.420
and all the things that have come out of that.

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:53.269
Working with the French Government, it will
take many weeks and months to get the full

00:13:53.269 --> 00:13:56.160
learning out of that; however, we will make
sure that, as any learning comes through,

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.910
it if applies to the NHS, we will relay it
to our front-line staff to make them even

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:01.970
more prepared.

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:03.560
Thank you, Barbara.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:09.059
That is a very comprehensive and, in many
ways, quite troubling report.

00:14:09.059 --> 00:14:11.050
Thank you for the assurance that you have
been able to give us.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:17.399
Let me invite any questions from around the
Board table.

00:14:17.399 --> 00:14:19.790
Back to 2.2 and 2.3.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:25.529
Attendances at A&amp;E up 0.6%, admissions up
2.1%.

00:14:25.529 --> 00:14:35.449
Is it the patients who are different, or is
it how we look after them that is different?

00:14:35.449 --> 00:14:42.300
I think the full answer is that we have got
an ageing population, they are seeing sicker

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:44.910
patients and more of them need admitting.

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:51.670
We have started to do quite a lot of communications
in the community to try and persuade people

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:54.660
not to go to A&amp;E if they do not need to.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:59.300
In a lot of places, the 111 service is really
helping and directing patients to primary

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:03.410
care, rather than to A&amp;E, if they do not need
to be admitted.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:09.220
I think we are beginning to see our A&amp;E services
protected for those patients who really need

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:10.220
them.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:16.399
What we are not yet doing sufficient of is
making inroads into the number of patients

00:15:16.399 --> 00:15:17.569
who need to be admitted.

00:15:17.569 --> 00:15:23.660
For that, we need to get more upstream in
the community.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:28.119
This is one of the particular worries for
us over Christmas and New Year, and a plea

00:15:28.119 --> 00:15:31.930
that we would want to make to patients, is
that often over the Christmas and New Year

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:37.939
period frail or elderly patients do not like
to be seen to be a trouble.

00:15:37.939 --> 00:15:42.600
They think that surgeries are not open over
that period, and that the doctors in the hospitals

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:43.600
are very busy.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.249
Therefore, they wait, and they get iller,
and then when we come back in the New Year

00:15:48.249 --> 00:15:53.429
or in the gap in between, these patients who
might at one stage have been able to be dealt

00:15:53.429 --> 00:15:55.839
with at home need to be admitted to hospital.

00:15:55.839 --> 00:16:03.129
There is still a lot of work to do, but this
is really a reflection of an ageing population

00:16:03.129 --> 00:16:04.800
for whom we can do much more.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:10.509
But if that trend goes forward, it is three
and a half times more admissions to additional

00:16:10.509 --> 00:16:11.600
people turning up.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:16.269
If you extrapolate that forward through the
five years we have just been talking about,

00:16:16.269 --> 00:16:19.689
it is quite a big problem.

00:16:19.689 --> 00:16:21.399
Well, we have done.

00:16:21.399 --> 00:16:24.990
That is what is in the base case for the demand
modelling, offset by the changes that we want

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:27.660
to introduce.

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:32.429
Nothing about this is inconsistent with the
decisions we have just taken.

00:16:32.429 --> 00:16:39.839
In fact, the rate of growth in emergency admissions
so far this year is lower than we were planning

00:16:39.839 --> 00:16:40.839
for.

00:16:40.839 --> 00:16:45.369
Simon, would that also be true of the growth
in delayed transfers of care, which is the

00:16:45.369 --> 00:16:48.389
most staggering growth in the whole of this
document?

00:16:48.389 --> 00:16:49.389
No.

00:16:49.389 --> 00:16:53.079
As Barbara says, it is putting pressure on.

00:16:53.079 --> 00:17:00.939
I do not think you can juxtapose the two and
say, ‘QED, therefore we have what we are

00:17:00.939 --> 00:17:04.130
seeing showing up as pressures on providers,
and there are some other things going on there

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:05.130
as well.’

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:12.190
The detox issue is obviously a canary down
the mine on the social care issue, and the

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:16.070
need for integrated and joint working.

00:17:16.070 --> 00:17:23.000
I think we have more work to do, frankly,
to land the right incentives between health

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.190
and social care on that question.

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:29.630
There is quite a lot of discussion going on
nationally on that topic.

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:34.210
I think it needs to be seen in perspective,
because the number of patients we discharge

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:40.760
every day, compared to the ones who are delayed,
is still a relatively small number.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:45.280
As well as working with social colleagues,
we need to redouble our efforts in the NHS,

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:50.030
because some of the delayed transfers of care
are because we have not necessarily got the

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:54.680
right intermediate or community care beds,
or the right services in the community.

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:58.130
Sometimes that is because those patients in
our intermediate or community care beds are

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:02.540
waiting for social care beds or care in the
community, so it really is a whole-system

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:05.740
approach, and it does matter.

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:06.740
It matters to patients.

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:13.750
It is not just about the beds and the funding;
it matters to patients to be able to go home

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:24.430
or to their place of care and have the right
arrangements in place for them.

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:40.380
In connection with paragraph 2.26, the IAPT
paragraph, you 

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:54.990
have referred to 
the fact that we are working on reducing variation

00:18:54.990 --> 00:19:03.000
in recovery rates, with certain support given
to the lowest-performing IAPT providers.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:07.480
I was just wondering whether, as a Board,
there was something that we could track that

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:15.960
would focus in particular on the fact that
this is linked to high levels of deprivation.

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:27.380
I was quite keen to see that we could actually
help to monitor some degree of improvement.

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:35.760
You are talking about some of the hardest
to reach people in that category.

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:41.540
I was just wondering whether there was some
metric that we could be monitoring?

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:49.770
I certainly do not think we have done that,
but Bruce’s team oversees IAPT for us.

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:52.440
Of course, the operations team help with the
numbers.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:55.560
We could take that back, could we not, Bruce,
and have a look and see.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:57.570
I think it is a good point.

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:58.720
Thank you very much.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:19:59.720
Right.

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.670
Well, thank you very much, Barbara, as always,
for that very clear report.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:09.740
I ask the Board just to note the report and
take assurance from it that we have identified

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:14.140
where there are risks, we have identified
where performance is weak, but we have also

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:16.750
heard of the mitigation measures.

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:22.190
Particularly, Barbara, the steps that you
are proposing to support the service through

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:31.030
the holiday period, and in the event of any
emergency which calls on our emergency resilience

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.030
capacity.

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:32.040
Thank you very much.

